-- agv-wave-soldering-pallet-transport.md
INSERT INTO posts (id, title, slug, excerpt, cover_image, content_markdown, content_html, content_text, status, source, featured, pinned, comments_enabled, seo_title, seo_description, canonical_url, robots, structured_data, i18n, published_at, created_at, updated_at, author_id, series_id)
VALUES ('post-agv-wave-line', 'One trolley, one operator less: the $2,500 answer to a wave line traffic jam', 'agv-wave-soldering-pallet-transport', 'Our SAGV30C return trolley costs about two months of one operator''s wage and removes the walking from wave soldering pallet transport. Here is when an AGV earns its keep — and when it does not.', '/images/wave-soldering-line-2.jpg', 'Watch a manual wave soldering line for ten minutes and you will see the real cost driver: people walking. An operator carries a pallet of boards to the machine, waits, unloads, carries the empty pallet back, then walks for more boards. The furnace is never the bottleneck of a manual wave line — the feet are. Our answer has been embarrassingly simple: a wireless return trolley that does the walking.

## The arithmetic I use before recommending any AGV

The SAGV30C-class cart we sell costs about **US$2,500 — roughly two months of one operator''s wage** in most of the markets we serve. That anchor is deliberate. Before any automation conversation, I ask three questions:

1. **How many kilometers does that operator walk per shift?** Pallet runs on a wave line routinely eat 5–8 km of walking. That is the job description nobody wrote.
2. **What breaks when they stop walking?** On most lines: nothing. The pallets still arrive, faster and on schedule.
3. **What is the alternative capital?** Rebuilding the line into a rotary conveyor system costs an order of magnitude more and stops production for weeks. The trolley solution is designed to retrofit a running line in a lunch break, not a shutdown.

If your answers make the cart replace one labor position — which is the design intent, one unit per one walking job — payback is measured in months, and the [output effect our AGV catalog documents](https://file.autoinsertion.com/public/Southern%20Machinery%20Product/AGV%20Southern%20Machinery.pdf) (we have measured runs showing 10%+ throughput on constrained wave lines) is a bonus, not the promise.

## What the machine actually does

The SFY03/SAGV family runs wireless on rechargeable battery, follows a set route between the loader, furnace, and unloader positions, and returns empty pallets on its own. Route programming is a 30-minute job — you can see the [origin setting procedure](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4xlA0cngF74) and an [AGV moving PCB magazines between stations](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SMa7k7QxGzg) on the channel. For the wave zone specifically, here is [an AGV transporting soldering pallets](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hFUWp8dT0t0) at work.

## When an AGV does NOT earn its keep

I tell customers no more often than you would expect:

- **One-off moves.** If pallets travel twice an hour, a person is cheaper. AGVs win on rhythm.
- **Mazes.** The line must have a repeatable route. If your layout changes monthly, fix the layout first.
- **Expecting headcount deletion on day one.** The honest first win is removing the walking from the job, then reassigning the person to inspection or kitting. The wage math still works; the org chart just needs honesty.

## The loop this starts

Once one trolley is accepted on the floor, the same wireless logic spreads: magazine racks to loaders, loaders to furnaces, furnaces to depaneling. That is how a manual line becomes a smart line without a demolition budget — one route at a time. The [SFY03 technical handbook](https://file.autoinsertion.com/public/SMThelp%20Machine%20Presentation/SFY03_Magnetic_AGV_Technical_Handbook.pdf) documents the route and safety behavior in full.

---

**Your move:** send me a floor plan and the walking distance of one shift. I will tell you whether a trolley earns its keep on YOUR line — and if not, I will say so in writing. [WhatsApp +86 13602562576](https://wa.me/8613602562576) · [info@smthelp.com](mailto:info@smthelp.com).

_— Jason Wu, Southern Machinery. Your Trusted Partner for SMT & THT Solutions._', '<p>Watch a manual wave soldering line for ten minutes and you will see the real cost driver: people walking. An operator carries a pallet of boards to the machine, waits, unloads, carries the empty pallet back, then walks for more boards. The furnace is never the bottleneck of a manual wave line — the feet are. Our answer has been embarrassingly simple: a wireless return trolley that does the walking.</p>
